# JSON

An RFC 8259 JSON value type, strict parser, and canonical serializer in pure LOGOS,
compiled through every execution tier. Auto-imported on reference.

The locked profile (see `json_conformance_lock.rs`):
numbers are exact scaled decimals (integer-valued numbers become `JNum`, non-integer
values become `JDec` = `mant × 10^scale` with `scale < 0` and `mant` not divisible by
10; a mantissa beyond i64 is rejected as out of range — no floats, no silent
precision loss); duplicate object keys keep the FIRST position with the LAST value;
strings decode all eight standard escapes plus `\uXXXX` (surrogate pairs decode to
their supplementary code point, lone surrogates are rejected); whitespace between
tokens is exactly space/tab/LF/CR; anything else — trailing input, unterminated
strings or containers, bare words, control characters in strings, leading zeros,
lone minus — is rejected with a positioned message.

## Note
A recursive JSON value: null, boolean, exact integer or decimal, string, array, or object.

A recursive JSON value: null, a boolean, an exact integer, an exact scaled decimal
(`mant × 10^scale`), a string, an array of `Json` values, or an object (a sequence
of `JEntry` key/value pairs in first-appearance order).

## A Json is either:
    A JNull.
    A JBool (b: Bool).
    A JNum (n: Int).
    A JDec with mant Int and scale Int.
    A JStr (s: Text).
    A JArr (elems: Seq of Json).
    A JObj (entries: Seq of JEntry).

## Note
One object member: a Text `key` and its `Json` `val`.

## A JEntry has:
    a key, which is Text.
    a val, which is Json.

## Note
The strict parse outcome: `JOk` carries the parsed value, `JErr` carries a
positioned error message (1-based byte positions).

## A JsonResult is either:
    A JOk (v: Json).
    A JErr (msg: Text).

## Note
Parser state threaded through the recursive descent: the parsed `node`, `nextpos`
(the byte position just past what was consumed), and the `ok`/`err` error channel.

## A ParseState has:
    a node, which is Json.
    a nextpos, which is Int.
    an ok, which is Bool.
    an err, which is Text.

## Note
A successful step: wraps `nd` and the next position.

## To jsonOkAt (nd: Json) and (p: Int) -> ParseState:
    Return a new ParseState with node nd and nextpos p and ok true and err "".

## Note
A failed step: carries the diagnostic message.

## To jsonFailAt (msg: Text) -> ParseState:
    Return a new ParseState with node (a new JNull) and nextpos 0 and ok false and err msg.

## Note
Advances past RFC whitespace (space 32, tab 9, LF 10, CR 13) from `pos`, returning
the first non-whitespace position.

## To jsonSkipWs (bytes: Seq of Int) and (pos: Int) -> Int:
    Let total be length of bytes.
    Let mutable p be pos.
    Let mutable go be true.
    While go is equal to true:
        If p is greater than total:
            Set go to false.
        Otherwise:
            Let b be item p of bytes.
            If b is equal to 32:
                Set p to p + 1.
            Otherwise:
                If b is equal to 9:
                    Set p to p + 1.
                Otherwise:
                    If b is equal to 10:
                        Set p to p + 1.
                    Otherwise:
                        If b is equal to 13:
                            Set p to p + 1.
                        Otherwise:
                            Set go to false.
    Return p.

## Note
True when the bytes at `pos` match `word` exactly, in bounds.

## To jsonMatchWord (bytes: Seq of Int) and (pos: Int) and (word: Seq of Int) -> Bool:
    Let total be length of bytes.
    Let k be length of word.
    If ((pos + k) - 1) is greater than total:
        Return false.
    Let mutable i be 1.
    While i is at most k:
        If item ((pos + i) - 1) of bytes is equal to item i of word:
            Set i to i + 1.
        Otherwise:
            Return false.
    Return true.

## Note
The value of one hex digit byte (`0-9`/`A-F`/`a-f`), or -1 when not a hex digit.

## To jsonHexDigit (b: Int) -> Int:
    If b is at least 48:
        If b is at most 57:
            Return b - 48.
    If b is at least 65:
        If b is at most 70:
            Return b - 55.
    If b is at least 97:
        If b is at most 102:
            Return b - 87.
    Return 0 - 1.

## Note
Reads four hex digit bytes starting at `pos` into their value, or -1 when out of
bounds or not hex.

## To jsonHex4 (bytes: Seq of Int) and (pos: Int) -> Int:
    Let total be length of bytes.
    If (pos + 3) is greater than total:
        Return 0 - 1.
    Let mutable v be 0.
    Let mutable i be 0.
    While i is at most 3:
        Let d be jsonHexDigit(item (pos + i) of bytes).
        If d is less than 0:
            Return 0 - 1.
        Set v to (v * 16) + d.
        Set i to i + 1.
    Return v.

## Note
The UTF-8 encoding of code point `cp` (1 to 4 bytes).

## To jsonUtf8Bytes (cp: Int) -> Seq of Int:
    Let out be a new Seq of Int.
    If cp is less than 128:
        Push cp to out.
        Return out.
    If cp is less than 2048:
        Push (192 + (cp / 64)) to out.
        Push (128 + (cp % 64)) to out.
        Return out.
    If cp is less than 65536:
        Push (224 + (cp / 4096)) to out.
        Push (128 + ((cp / 64) % 64)) to out.
        Push (128 + (cp % 64)) to out.
        Return out.
    Push (240 + (cp / 262144)) to out.
    Push (128 + ((cp / 4096) % 64)) to out.
    Push (128 + ((cp / 64) % 64)) to out.
    Push (128 + (cp % 64)) to out.
    Return out.

## Note
Parses the `"..."` string whose opening quote is at `pos` into a `JStr`.

Decodes the eight standard escapes and `\uXXXX` (surrogate pairs included, lone surrogates rejected), and rejects raw control bytes.
## To jsonParseString (bytes: Seq of Int) and (pos: Int) -> ParseState:
    Let total be length of bytes.
    Let out be a new Seq of Int.
    Let mutable p be pos + 1.
    Let mutable go be true.
    While go is equal to true:
        If p is greater than total:
            Return jsonFailAt("unterminated string starting at byte {pos}").
        Let b be item p of bytes.
        If b is equal to 34:
            Let txt be text_from_bytes(out).
            Let nd be a new JStr with s txt.
            Return jsonOkAt(nd, p + 1).
        If b is equal to 92:
            If (p + 1) is greater than total:
                Return jsonFailAt("unterminated string starting at byte {pos}").
            Let c be item (p + 1) of bytes.
            Let mutable simple be 0 - 1.
            If c is equal to 34:
                Set simple to 34.
            If c is equal to 92:
                Set simple to 92.
            If c is equal to 47:
                Set simple to 47.
            If c is equal to 98:
                Set simple to 8.
            If c is equal to 102:
                Set simple to 12.
            If c is equal to 110:
                Set simple to 10.
            If c is equal to 114:
                Set simple to 13.
            If c is equal to 116:
                Set simple to 9.
            If simple is at least 0:
                Push simple to out.
                Set p to p + 2.
            Otherwise:
                If c is equal to 117:
                    Let hi be jsonHex4(bytes, p + 2).
                    If hi is less than 0:
                        Return jsonFailAt("invalid unicode escape at byte {p}").
                    Let mutable ishi be false.
                    If hi is at least 55296:
                        If hi is at most 56319:
                            Set ishi to true.
                    Let mutable islo be false.
                    If hi is at least 56320:
                        If hi is at most 57343:
                            Set islo to true.
                    If islo is equal to true:
                        Return jsonFailAt("lone surrogate at byte {p}").
                    If ishi is equal to true:
                        Let mutable pair be false.
                        If (p + 7) is at most total:
                            If item (p + 6) of bytes is equal to 92:
                                If item (p + 7) of bytes is equal to 117:
                                    Set pair to true.
                        If pair is equal to false:
                            Return jsonFailAt("lone surrogate at byte {p}").
                        Let lo be jsonHex4(bytes, p + 8).
                        If lo is less than 56320:
                            Return jsonFailAt("lone surrogate at byte {p}").
                        If lo is greater than 57343:
                            Return jsonFailAt("lone surrogate at byte {p}").
                        Let cp be (65536 + ((hi - 55296) * 1024)) + (lo - 56320).
                        Let eb be jsonUtf8Bytes(cp).
                        Let ebn be length of eb.
                        Let mutable k be 1.
                        While k is at most ebn:
                            Push item k of eb to out.
                            Set k to k + 1.
                        Set p to p + 12.
                    Otherwise:
                        Let eb2 be jsonUtf8Bytes(hi).
                        Let ebn2 be length of eb2.
                        Let mutable k2 be 1.
                        While k2 is at most ebn2:
                            Push item k2 of eb2 to out.
                            Set k2 to k2 + 1.
                        Set p to p + 6.
                Otherwise:
                    Return jsonFailAt("invalid escape at byte {p}").
        Otherwise:
            If b is less than 32:
                Return jsonFailAt("control character in string at byte {p}").
            Push b to out.
            Set p to p + 1.
    Return jsonFailAt("unterminated string starting at byte {pos}").

## Note
Parses an RFC number at `pos`: optional minus, integer part (leading zeros rejected), optional fraction, optional exponent — folded EXACTLY into `mant × 10^scale`.

Integer-valued results normalize to `JNum`; others to canonical `JDec` (`scale <
0`, `mant` not divisible by 10). A mantissa beyond i64 is out of range.
## To jsonParseNumber (bytes: Seq of Int) and (pos: Int) -> ParseState:
    Let total be length of bytes.
    Let mutable p be pos.
    Let mutable neg be false.
    If item p of bytes is equal to 45:
        Set neg to true.
        Set p to p + 1.
    If p is greater than total:
        Return jsonFailAt("unexpected end of input").
    Let first be item p of bytes.
    If first is less than 48:
        Return jsonFailAt("invalid number at byte {p}").
    If first is greater than 57:
        Return jsonFailAt("invalid number at byte {p}").
    Let mutable m be 0.
    If first is equal to 48:
        Set p to p + 1.
        If p is at most total:
            Let d2 be item p of bytes.
            If d2 is at least 48:
                If d2 is at most 57:
                    Return jsonFailAt("invalid number at byte {p}").
    Otherwise:
        Let mutable igo be true.
        While igo is equal to true:
            If p is greater than total:
                Set igo to false.
            Otherwise:
                Let d be item p of bytes.
                If d is at least 48:
                    If d is at most 57:
                        If m is greater than 922337203685477580:
                            Return jsonFailAt("number out of range at byte {pos}").
                        If m is equal to 922337203685477580:
                            If d is greater than 55:
                                Return jsonFailAt("number out of range at byte {pos}").
                        Set m to (m * 10) + (d - 48).
                        Set p to p + 1.
                    Otherwise:
                        Set igo to false.
                Otherwise:
                    Set igo to false.
    Let mutable sc be 0.
    If p is at most total:
        If item p of bytes is equal to 46:
            Set p to p + 1.
            If p is greater than total:
                Return jsonFailAt("unexpected end of input").
            Let fd be item p of bytes.
            If fd is less than 48:
                Return jsonFailAt("invalid number at byte {p}").
            If fd is greater than 57:
                Return jsonFailAt("invalid number at byte {p}").
            Let mutable fgo be true.
            While fgo is equal to true:
                If p is greater than total:
                    Set fgo to false.
                Otherwise:
                    Let d3 be item p of bytes.
                    If d3 is at least 48:
                        If d3 is at most 57:
                            If m is greater than 922337203685477580:
                                Return jsonFailAt("number out of range at byte {pos}").
                            If m is equal to 922337203685477580:
                                If d3 is greater than 55:
                                    Return jsonFailAt("number out of range at byte {pos}").
                            Set m to (m * 10) + (d3 - 48).
                            Set sc to sc - 1.
                            Set p to p + 1.
                        Otherwise:
                            Set fgo to false.
                    Otherwise:
                        Set fgo to false.
    If p is at most total:
        Let eb be item p of bytes.
        Let mutable isexp be false.
        If eb is equal to 101:
            Set isexp to true.
        If eb is equal to 69:
            Set isexp to true.
        If isexp is equal to true:
            Set p to p + 1.
            Let mutable esign be 1.
            If p is at most total:
                Let sb be item p of bytes.
                If sb is equal to 43:
                    Set p to p + 1.
                Otherwise:
                    If sb is equal to 45:
                        Set esign to 0 - 1.
                        Set p to p + 1.
            If p is greater than total:
                Return jsonFailAt("unexpected end of input").
            Let ed be item p of bytes.
            If ed is less than 48:
                Return jsonFailAt("invalid number at byte {p}").
            If ed is greater than 57:
                Return jsonFailAt("invalid number at byte {p}").
            Let mutable ev be 0.
            Let mutable ego be true.
            While ego is equal to true:
                If p is greater than total:
                    Set ego to false.
                Otherwise:
                    Let d4 be item p of bytes.
                    If d4 is at least 48:
                        If d4 is at most 57:
                            If ev is less than 10000000:
                                Set ev to (ev * 10) + (d4 - 48).
                            Set p to p + 1.
                        Otherwise:
                            Set ego to false.
                    Otherwise:
                        Set ego to false.
            Set sc to sc + (esign * ev).
    If m is equal to 0:
        Set sc to 0.
    Let mutable sgo be true.
    While sgo is equal to true:
        If sc is less than 0:
            If (m % 10) is equal to 0:
                Set m to m / 10.
                Set sc to sc + 1.
            Otherwise:
                Set sgo to false.
        Otherwise:
            Set sgo to false.
    Let mutable ugo be true.
    While ugo is equal to true:
        If sc is greater than 0:
            If m is greater than 922337203685477580:
                Return jsonFailAt("number out of range at byte {pos}").
            Set m to m * 10.
            Set sc to sc - 1.
        Otherwise:
            Set ugo to false.
    If neg is equal to true:
        Set m to 0 - m.
    If sc is equal to 0:
        Let nd be a new JNum with n m.
        Return jsonOkAt(nd, p).
    Let nd2 be a new JDec with mant m and scale sc.
    Return jsonOkAt(nd2, p).

## Note
Parses a `[...]` array at `pos`: values separated by commas until `]`, each element parsed by `jsonParseValue`.

`strict` (0 lenient, 1 strict) is threaded to every element so duplicate-key
rejection reaches nested objects.
## To jsonParseArray (bytes: Seq of Int) and (pos: Int) and (strict: Int) -> ParseState:
    Let total be length of bytes.
    Let items be a new Seq of Json.
    Let mutable p be jsonSkipWs(bytes, pos + 1).
    If p is greater than total:
        Return jsonFailAt("unexpected end of input").
    If item p of bytes is equal to 93:
        Let nd be a new JArr with elems items.
        Return jsonOkAt(nd, p + 1).
    Let mutable go be true.
    While go is equal to true:
        Let st be jsonParseValue(bytes, p, strict).
        If st's ok is equal to false:
            Return st.
        Push st's node to items.
        Set p to jsonSkipWs(bytes, st's nextpos).
        If p is greater than total:
            Return jsonFailAt("unexpected end of input").
        Let b be item p of bytes.
        If b is equal to 44:
            Set p to p + 1.
        Otherwise:
            If b is equal to 93:
                Let nd2 be a new JArr with elems items.
                Return jsonOkAt(nd2, p + 1).
            Otherwise:
                Return jsonFailAt("expected a comma or closing bracket at byte {p}").
    Return jsonFailAt("unexpected end of input").

## Note
Parses a `{...}` object at `pos`: string keys, colons, values, commas until the closing brace.

In the LENIENT mode (`strict` 0) duplicate keys keep the FIRST position with the
LAST value; in STRICT mode (`strict` 1) a duplicate key is REJECTED with a
positioned message (the byte of the offending key's opening quote).
## To jsonParseObject (bytes: Seq of Int) and (pos: Int) and (strict: Int) -> ParseState:
    Let total be length of bytes.
    Let es be a new Seq of JEntry.
    Let mutable p be jsonSkipWs(bytes, pos + 1).
    If p is greater than total:
        Return jsonFailAt("unexpected end of input").
    If item p of bytes is equal to 125:
        Let nd be a new JObj with entries es.
        Return jsonOkAt(nd, p + 1).
    Let mutable go be true.
    While go is equal to true:
        Set p to jsonSkipWs(bytes, p).
        If p is greater than total:
            Return jsonFailAt("unexpected end of input").
        If item p of bytes is equal to 34:
            Let kquote be p.
            Let kst be jsonParseString(bytes, p).
            If kst's ok is equal to false:
                Return kst.
            Let knode be kst's node.
            Let mutable kname be "".
            Inspect knode:
                When JStr (s):
                    Set kname to s.
                Otherwise:
                    Set kname to "".
            Set p to jsonSkipWs(bytes, kst's nextpos).
            If p is greater than total:
                Return jsonFailAt("unexpected end of input").
            If item p of bytes is equal to 58:
                Set p to p + 1.
            Otherwise:
                Return jsonFailAt("expected a colon at byte {p}").
            Let vst be jsonParseValue(bytes, p, strict).
            If vst's ok is equal to false:
                Return vst.
            Let vnode be vst's node.
            Let en be a new JEntry with key kname and val vnode.
            Let n be length of es.
            Let mutable i be 1.
            Let mutable found be false.
            While i is at most n:
                Let old be item i of es.
                If old's key is equal to kname:
                    Set item i of es to en.
                    Set found to true.
                    Set i to n + 1.
                Otherwise:
                    Set i to i + 1.
            If found is equal to true:
                If strict is equal to 1:
                    Return jsonFailAt("duplicate object key \"{kname}\" at byte {kquote}").
            If found is equal to false:
                Push en to es.
            Set p to jsonSkipWs(bytes, vst's nextpos).
            If p is greater than total:
                Return jsonFailAt("unexpected end of input").
            Let b be item p of bytes.
            If b is equal to 44:
                Set p to p + 1.
            Otherwise:
                If b is equal to 125:
                    Let nd2 be a new JObj with entries es.
                    Return jsonOkAt(nd2, p + 1).
                Otherwise:
                    Return jsonFailAt("expected a comma or closing brace at byte {p}").
        Otherwise:
            Return jsonFailAt("expected an object key at byte {p}").
    Return jsonFailAt("unexpected end of input").

## Note
Skips whitespace, then dispatches on the next byte: object, array, string, number, `true`, `false`, or `null` — anything else is rejected with its position.

`strict` (0 lenient, 1 strict) is threaded to the container arms so duplicate-
key rejection reaches every nested object.
## To jsonParseValue (bytes: Seq of Int) and (pos: Int) and (strict: Int) -> ParseState:
    Let total be length of bytes.
    Let p be jsonSkipWs(bytes, pos).
    If p is greater than total:
        Return jsonFailAt("unexpected end of input").
    Let c be item p of bytes.
    If c is equal to 123:
        Return jsonParseObject(bytes, p, strict).
    If c is equal to 91:
        Return jsonParseArray(bytes, p, strict).
    If c is equal to 34:
        Return jsonParseString(bytes, p).
    If c is equal to 45:
        Return jsonParseNumber(bytes, p).
    If c is at least 48:
        If c is at most 57:
            Return jsonParseNumber(bytes, p).
    If c is equal to 116:
        Let wt be [116, 114, 117, 101].
        If jsonMatchWord(bytes, p, wt) is equal to true:
            Let nd be a new JBool with b true.
            Return jsonOkAt(nd, p + 4).
        Return jsonFailAt("invalid literal at byte {p}").
    If c is equal to 102:
        Let wf be [102, 97, 108, 115, 101].
        If jsonMatchWord(bytes, p, wf) is equal to true:
            Let nd2 be a new JBool with b false.
            Return jsonOkAt(nd2, p + 5).
        Return jsonFailAt("invalid literal at byte {p}").
    If c is equal to 110:
        Let wn be [110, 117, 108, 108].
        If jsonMatchWord(bytes, p, wn) is equal to true:
            Let nd3 be a new JNull.
            Return jsonOkAt(nd3, p + 4).
        Return jsonFailAt("invalid literal at byte {p}").
    Return jsonFailAt("unexpected character at byte {p}").

## Note
Parses one complete document: a value, then only whitespace to the end — anything else is trailing input.

`strict` (0 lenient, 1 strict) is threaded to the value so duplicate object keys
are rejected when strict.
## To jsonParseDoc (bytes: Seq of Int) and (strict: Int) -> ParseState:
    Let st be jsonParseValue(bytes, 1, strict).
    If st's ok is equal to false:
        Return st.
    Let total be length of bytes.
    Let p be jsonSkipWs(bytes, st's nextpos).
    If p is at most total:
        Return jsonFailAt("trailing characters after the JSON value at byte {p}").
    Return st.

## Note
The non-trapping parser: `JOk` with the value, or `JErr` with a positioned message.

LENIENT on duplicate object keys — the last value wins, the first position is
kept (JS `JSON.parse` compatible). Use `jsonParseStrict` when a colliding key
must be an error rather than a silent last-write-wins.
## To jsonTryParse (text: Text) -> JsonResult:
    Let st be jsonParseDoc(text_bytes(text), 0).
    If st's ok is equal to true:
        Let nd be st's node.
        Return a new JOk with v nd.
    Let m be st's err.
    Return a new JErr with msg m.

## Note
The strict non-trapping parser: like `jsonTryParse`, but a duplicate object key anywhere in the document is rejected.

The rejection is a `JErr` with a positioned message rather than a silent collapse. The typed-decode path
uses this so no decision is ever taken on a document that dropped a colliding
key.
## To jsonParseStrict (text: Text) -> JsonResult:
    Let st be jsonParseDoc(text_bytes(text), 1).
    If st's ok is equal to true:
        Let nd be st's node.
        Return a new JOk with v nd.
    Let m be st's err.
    Return a new JErr with msg m.

## Note
The trapping parser: returns the value of a valid document and stops the program on an invalid one.

Use `jsonTryParse` to handle errors instead. Lenient on duplicate keys (last value wins).
## To jsonParse (text: Text) -> Json:
    Let st be jsonParseDoc(text_bytes(text), 0).
    Assert that st's ok is equal to true.
    Return st's node.

## Note
True iff `text` is one complete, well-formed JSON document (`encoding/json.Valid`); lenient on duplicate keys.

## To jsonValid (text: Text) -> Bool:
    Let st be jsonParseDoc(text_bytes(text), 0).
    Return st's ok.

## Note
Object member lookup: the value under `key`, or `JNull` when `j` is not an object
or has no such member (pair with `jsonHas` to tell a stored null from a missing key).

## To jsonGet (j: Json) and (key: Text) -> Json:
    Inspect j:
        When JObj (entries):
            Let n be length of entries.
            Let mutable i be 1.
            While i is at most n:
                Let en be item i of entries.
                If en's key is equal to key:
                    Return en's val.
                Set i to i + 1.
            Return a new JNull.
        Otherwise:
            Return a new JNull.
    Return a new JNull.

## Note
True when `j` is an object with a member under `key`.

## To jsonHas (j: Json) and (key: Text) -> Bool:
    Inspect j:
        When JObj (entries):
            Let n be length of entries.
            Let mutable i be 1.
            While i is at most n:
                Let en be item i of entries.
                If en's key is equal to key:
                    Return true.
                Set i to i + 1.
            Return false.
        Otherwise:
            Return false.
    Return false.

## Note
The lowercase hex digit byte for `v` in 0..15.

## To jsonHexByte (v: Int) -> Int:
    If v is less than 10:
        Return 48 + v.
    Return 87 + v.

## Note
Quotes and escapes a string body per the canonical profile: `"` and `\` escaped,
controls as `\b \f \n \r \t` or lowercase `\u00xx`; everything else (including raw
UTF-8) passes through.

## To jsonQuote (s: Text) -> Text:
    Let sb be text_bytes(s).
    Let n be length of sb.
    Let out be a new Seq of Int.
    Push 34 to out.
    Let mutable i be 1.
    While i is at most n:
        Let b be item i of sb.
        If b is equal to 34:
            Push 92 to out.
            Push 34 to out.
        Otherwise:
            If b is equal to 92:
                Push 92 to out.
                Push 92 to out.
            Otherwise:
                If b is less than 32:
                    Let mutable esc be 0 - 1.
                    If b is equal to 8:
                        Set esc to 98.
                    If b is equal to 12:
                        Set esc to 102.
                    If b is equal to 10:
                        Set esc to 110.
                    If b is equal to 13:
                        Set esc to 114.
                    If b is equal to 9:
                        Set esc to 116.
                    If esc is at least 0:
                        Push 92 to out.
                        Push esc to out.
                    Otherwise:
                        Push 92 to out.
                        Push 117 to out.
                        Push 48 to out.
                        Push 48 to out.
                        Let h1 be jsonHexByte(b / 16).
                        Let h2 be jsonHexByte(b % 16).
                        Push h1 to out.
                        Push h2 to out.
                Otherwise:
                    Push b to out.
        Set i to i + 1.
    Push 34 to out.
    Return text_from_bytes(out).

## Note
Renders a canonical `JDec` (`sc < 0`) in plain decimal notation, never exponent form.

The mantissa's digits get the point inserted, zero-padded as needed. A non-canonical `sc >= 0` renders as the
mantissa followed by `sc` zeros.
## To jsonRenderDec (m: Int) and (sc: Int) -> Text:
    Let mutable neg be false.
    Let mutable mag be m.
    If m is less than 0:
        Set neg to true.
        Set mag to 0 - m.
    Let dtxt be "{mag}".
    Let db be text_bytes(dtxt).
    Let n be length of db.
    Let out be a new Seq of Int.
    If neg is equal to true:
        Push 45 to out.
    If sc is at least 0:
        Let mutable i be 1.
        While i is at most n:
            Push item i of db to out.
            Set i to i + 1.
        Let mutable z be sc.
        While z is greater than 0:
            Push 48 to out.
            Set z to z - 1.
        Return text_from_bytes(out).
    Let k be 0 - sc.
    If n is greater than k:
        Let cut be n - k.
        Let mutable i2 be 1.
        While i2 is at most cut:
            Push item i2 of db to out.
            Set i2 to i2 + 1.
        Push 46 to out.
        Let mutable i3 be cut + 1.
        While i3 is at most n:
            Push item i3 of db to out.
            Set i3 to i3 + 1.
        Return text_from_bytes(out).
    Push 48 to out.
    Push 46 to out.
    Let mutable z2 be k - n.
    While z2 is greater than 0:
        Push 48 to out.
        Set z2 to z2 - 1.
    Let mutable i4 be 1.
    While i4 is at most n:
        Push item i4 of db to out.
        Set i4 to i4 + 1.
    Return text_from_bytes(out).

## Note
Serializes a `Json` value to its canonical JSON text: no whitespace, strings
escaped per profile, objects in stored member order, numbers in plain decimal.

## To jsonSerialize (j: Json) -> Text:
    Inspect j:
        When JNull:
            Return "null".
        When JBool (b):
            If b is equal to true:
                Return "true".
            Return "false".
        When JNum (n):
            Return "{n}".
        When JDec (dm, dsc):
            Return jsonRenderDec(dm, dsc).
        When JStr (s):
            Return jsonQuote(s).
        When JArr (elems):
            Let mutable out be "[".
            Let n2 be length of elems.
            Let mutable i be 1.
            While i is at most n2:
                Set out to out combined with jsonSerialize(item i of elems).
                If i is less than n2:
                    Set out to out combined with ",".
                Set i to i + 1.
            Return out combined with "]".
        When JObj (entries):
            Let mutable out2 be text_from_bytes([123]).
            Let n3 be length of entries.
            Let mutable i2 be 1.
            While i2 is at most n3:
                Let en be item i2 of entries.
                Let ek be en's key.
                Let ev be en's val.
                Set out2 to out2 combined with jsonQuote(ek).
                Set out2 to out2 combined with ":".
                Set out2 to out2 combined with jsonSerialize(ev).
                If i2 is less than n3:
                    Set out2 to out2 combined with ",".
                Set i2 to i2 + 1.
            Return out2 combined with text_from_bytes([125]).
    Return "null".

## Note
Typed structured decoding.

Every extractor returns a `Result` (`Ok value` / `Err message`): the right
type/shape decodes, the wrong one is REJECTED — never silently defaulted. A
caller composes these to read a document into typed fields, so no authorization
or money decision is ever made on an untyped JSON field.
## Note
The Text inside a `JStr`, or `Err` when `v` is any other JSON type.

## To jsonString (v: Json) -> Result of Text and Text:
    Inspect v:
        When JStr (s):
            Return a new Ok with value s.
        Otherwise:
            Return a new Err with error "expected a JSON string".
    Return a new Err with error "expected a JSON string".

## Note
The Int inside a `JNum`, or `Err` when `v` is any other JSON type (a non-integer
`JDec` is NOT an integer — it is rejected, never truncated).

## To jsonInt (v: Json) -> Result of Int and Text:
    Inspect v:
        When JNum (n):
            Return a new Ok with value n.
        Otherwise:
            Return a new Err with error "expected a JSON integer".
    Return a new Err with error "expected a JSON integer".

## Note
The Bool inside a `JBool`, or `Err` when `v` is any other JSON type.

## To jsonBool (v: Json) -> Result of Bool and Text:
    Inspect v:
        When JBool (b):
            Return a new Ok with value b.
        Otherwise:
            Return a new Err with error "expected a JSON boolean".
    Return a new Err with error "expected a JSON boolean".

## Note
The element sequence inside a `JArr`, or `Err` when `v` is any other JSON type.

## To jsonArray (v: Json) -> Result of Seq of Json and Text:
    Inspect v:
        When JArr (elems):
            Return a new Ok with value elems.
        Otherwise:
            Return a new Err with error "expected a JSON array".
    Return a new Err with error "expected a JSON array".

## Note
The member sequence inside a `JObj`, or `Err` when `v` is any other JSON type. Use
this to REQUIRE an object shape before reading fields.

## To jsonRequireObject (v: Json) -> Result of Seq of JEntry and Text:
    Inspect v:
        When JObj (entries):
            Return a new Ok with value entries.
        Otherwise:
            Return a new Err with error "expected a JSON object".
    Return a new Err with error "expected a JSON object".

## Note
The value under `key`, or `Err "missing field …"` when `v` is not an object or has no such member.

Unlike `jsonGet` (which returns `JNull` for a missing key), this distinguishes
ABSENT from a stored null: an absent required field is a REJECTION.
## To jsonField (v: Json) and (key: Text) -> Result of Json and Text:
    Inspect v:
        When JObj (entries):
            Let n be length of entries.
            Let mutable i be 1.
            While i is at most n:
                Let en be item i of entries.
                If en's key is equal to key:
                    Return a new Ok with value (en's val).
                Set i to i + 1.
            Return a new Err with error "missing field \"{key}\"".
        Otherwise:
            Return a new Err with error "expected a JSON object".
    Return a new Err with error "expected a JSON object".

## Note
`Ok true` when every key of the object `v` is present in `allowed`; `Err "unexpected key …"` on the FIRST key that is not (positioned on the name, not the byte).

Pairs with `jsonField` to enforce a CLOSED shape — extra keys are rejected,
never ignored.
## To jsonNoExtraKeys (v: Json) and (allowed: Seq of Text) -> Result of Bool and Text:
    Inspect v:
        When JObj (entries):
            Let n be length of entries.
            Let m be length of allowed.
            Let mutable i be 1.
            While i is at most n:
                Let en be item i of entries.
                Let ek be en's key.
                Let mutable ok be false.
                Let mutable j be 1.
                While j is at most m:
                    If item j of allowed is equal to ek:
                        Set ok to true.
                        Set j to m + 1.
                    Otherwise:
                        Set j to j + 1.
                If ok is equal to false:
                    Return a new Err with error "unexpected key \"{ek}\"".
                Set i to i + 1.
            Return a new Ok with value true.
        Otherwise:
            Return a new Err with error "expected a JSON object".
    Return a new Err with error "expected a JSON object".

## Note
Whether this value is JSON `null`.

`jsonGet` answers `JNull` for a key an object does not carry, so this is true for an
explicit `null` AND for an absent key — the two a reader of an OPTIONAL field treats
alike, since both mean the sender has nothing there.

## To jsonIsNull (v: Json) -> Bool:
    Inspect v:
        When JNull:
            Return true.
        Otherwise:
            Return false.
    Return false.
